<ecoSpold xmlns="http://www.EcoInvent.org/EcoSpold02">
  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="ef9a33e0-0ede-52ed-b093-f38b073602d0" activityNameId="9432435b-c786-54a9-8430-554fb3fe2bbf" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Transport, freight, rail</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Transport, freight, rail
Inventory refers to the entire transport life cycle. For rail infrastructure, expenditures and environmental interventions due to construction, renewal and disposal of rail tracks have been allocated based on the Gross tonne kilometre performance (39674 Mio. Gtkm).  Expenditures due to operation of the rail infrastructure, as well as land use have been allocated based on the yearly train kilometre performance (12757 Mio. tkm).  For the operation of goods transportation average data by rail in Italy were used. The data has been determined top down, by combining the total yearly energy consumption and the yearly performance of goods transportation.;
UUID: 58a7e50f-91e8-3981-a720-e766e6996848
The inventory includes the actual energy consumption for an average goods train  in Italy using electro and diesel energy. In addition the diesel consumption and emissions of shunting processes are accounted for  as well as the production and maintenance of vehicles and railyway tracks.
Location: &lt;null&gt;
Technology: Average technology
Time period: Time of publications
Production volume: 12757Mio. tkm
Sampling: Statistics 
Extrapolations: For the determination of the train and rail track demand per tkm date of Switzerland was used and extrapolated with the tkm of IT
Uncertainty adjustments: None
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_58a7e50f-91e8-3981-a720-e766e6996848.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
